{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Please forgive the transgression of your brothers and their sin, for they repaid you with evil.</b> He should have said 'Please forgive the transgression and sin of your brothers' — so why did he insert 'your brothers' between the two joined nouns? And what explanation is given by saying 'for they repaid you with evil'? Now it appears that there were here both a transgression and a sin. The transgression is that they sought to kill him by hand, and he is not liable to the death penalty at human hands even according to their own view, for they considered him a slanderer. The sin is that they sold him into slavery, and they have a certain defense for this, for they could say: since he brought an evil report about them, saying that they call the sons of the maidservants 'slaves,' therefore they treated him in kind and sold him into slavery. Therefore it says 'Please forgive — for the transgression of your brothers,' because for the transgression they have no defense whatsoever; therefore they seek pardon and mercy. But 'and their sin' — justice demands that you forgive it, 'for they repaid you with evil,' meaning that they have a certain defense, for that evil which they repaid you was namely the evil report. And by this it is settled why he placed the transgression before the sin, for this is not the way of supplicants — to ask first about the greater matter and afterward about the lesser; the opposite should be required. But according to our explanation it is settled."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:41.478029123Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}